            (<span lang="zh">梁山柏</span>)</span><div class="c-article-authors-search__list"><div class="c-article-authors-search__item c-article-authors-search__list-item--left"><a href="/search?author=Shanbai%20Liang" class="c-article-button" data-track="click" data-track-action="author link - publication" data-track-label="link" rel="nofollow">View author publications</a></div><div class="c-article-authors-search__item c-article-authors-search__list-item--right"><p class="search-in-title-js c-article-authors-search__text"><span class="c-article-authors-search__links-text">Search author on:</span><span class="c-article-identifiers"><a class="c-article-identifiers__item" href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=search&amp;term=Shanbai%20Liang" data-track="click" data-track-action="author link - pubmed" data-track-label="link" rel="nofollow">PubMed</a><span class="u-hide"> </span><a class="c-article-identifiers__item" href="https://scholar.google.co.uk/scholar?as_q=&amp;num=10&amp;btnG=Search+Scholar&amp;as_epq=&amp;as_oq=&amp;as_eq=&amp;as_occt=any&amp;as_sauthors=%22Shanbai%20Liang%22&amp;as_publication=&amp;as_ylo=&amp;as_yhi=&amp;as_allsubj=all&amp;hl=en" data-track="click" data-track-action="author link - scholar" data-track-label="link" rel="nofollow">Google Scholar</a></span></p></div></div></li><li id="auth-Li-Guo-Aff3"><span class="c-article-authors-search__title u-h3 js-search-name">Li Guo 
            (<span lang="zh">郭立</span>)</span><div class="c-article-authors-search__list"><div class="c-article-authors-search__item c-article-authors-search__list-item--left"><a href="/search?author=Li%20Guo" class="c-article-button" data-track="click" data-track-action="author link - publication" data-track-label="link" rel="nofollow">View author publications</a></div><div class="c-article-authors-search__item c-article-authors-search__list-item--right"><p class="search-in-title-js c-article-authors-search__text"><span class="c-article-authors-search__links-text">Search author on:</span><span class="c-article-identifiers"><a class="c-article-identifiers__item" href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=search&amp;term=Li%20Guo" data-track="click" data-track-action="author link - pubmed" data-track-label="link" rel="nofollow">PubMed</a><span class="u-hide"> </span><a class="c-article-identifiers__item" href="https://scholar.google.co.uk/scholar?as_q=&amp;num=10&amp;btnG=Search+Scholar&amp;as_epq=&amp;as_oq=&amp;as_eq=&amp;as_occt=any&amp;as_sauthors=%22Li%20Guo%22&amp;as_publication=&amp;as_ylo=&amp;as_yhi=&amp;as_allsubj=all&amp;hl=en" data-track="click" data-track-action="author link - scholar" data-track-label="link" rel="nofollow">Google Scholar</a></span></p></div></div></li><li id="auth-Dawen-Yang-Aff1-Aff2"><span class="c-article-authors-search__title u-h3 js-search-name">Dawen Yang 
            (<span lang="zh">杨大文</span>)</span><div class="c-article-authors-search__list"><div class="c-article-authors-search__item c-article-authors-search__list-item--left"><a href="/search?author=Dawen%20Yang" class="c-article-button" data-track="click" data-track-action="author link - publication" data-track-label="link" rel="nofollow">View author publications</a></div><div class="c-article-authors-search__item c-article-authors-search__list-item--right"><p class="search-in-title-js c-article-authors-search__text"><span class="c-article-authors-search__links-text">Search author on:</span><span class="c-article-identifiers"><a class="c-article-identifiers__item" href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=search&amp;term=Dawen%20Yang" data-track="click" data-track-action="author link - pubmed" data-track-label="link" rel="nofollow">PubMed</a><span class="u-hide"> </span><a class="c-article-identifiers__item" href="https://scholar.google.co.uk/scholar?as_q=&amp;num=10&amp;btnG=Search+Scholar&amp;as_epq=&amp;as_oq=&amp;as_eq=&amp;as_occt=any&amp;as_sauthors=%22Dawen%20Yang%22&amp;as_publication=&amp;as_ylo=&amp;as_yhi=&amp;as_allsubj=all&amp;hl=en" data-track="click" data-track-action="author link - scholar" data-track-label="link" rel="nofollow">Google Scholar</a></span></p></div></div></li></ol></div><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="contributions">Contributions</h3><p>Y.Y. initiated the idea and designed the study with D.Y. and Y.G. Y.G., J.X. and Y.Y. performed the analyses with help from S.L. and L.G. Y.Y. and Y.G. drafted the paper. All authors contributed to the results discussion and the review and editing of the paper. Y.Y. and D.Y. acquired funding for this research.</p><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="corresponding-author">Corresponding authors</h3><p id="corresponding-author-list">Correspondence to
                <a id="corresp-c1" aria-label="email Yuting Yang 杨雨亭" href="mailto:yuting_yang@tsinghua.edu.cn">Yuting Yang 
            (<span lang="zh">杨雨亭</span>)</a> or <a id="corresp-c2" aria-label="email Dawen Yang 杨大文" href="mailto:yangdw@tsinghua.edu.cn">Dawen Yang 
            (<span lang="zh">杨大文</span>)</a>.</p></div></div></section><section data-title="Ethics declarations"><div class="c-article-section" id="ethics-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="ethics">Ethics declarations</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="ethics-content">
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar8">Competing interests</h3>
              <p>The authors declare no competing interests.</p>
            
          </div></div></section><section data-title="Peer review"><div class="c-article-section" id="peer-review-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="peer-review">Peer review</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="peer-review-content">
            
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar7">Peer review information</h3>
              <p><i>Nature Water</i> thanks Xiaomang Liu and the other anonymous reviewer(s) for their contribution to the peer review of this work.</p>
            
          </div></div></section><section data-title="Additional information"><div class="c-article-section" id="additional-information-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="additional-information">Additional information</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="additional-information-content"><p><b>Publisher’s note</b> Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.</p></div></div></section><section data-title="Extended data"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec21-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec21">Extended data</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec21-content"><div data-test="supplementary-info"><div id="figshareContainer" class="c-article-figshare-container" data-test="figshare-container"></div><div class="c-article-supplementary__item js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="supp-item" id="Fig4"><h3 class="c-article-supplementary__title u-h3"><a data-test="supp-info-link" class="print-link" data-track="call_to_action" data-track-action="view supplementary info" data-track-context="Extended data" data-track-label="extended data fig. 1 spatiotemporal changes in hyd" href="/articles/s44221-026-00724-8/figures/4" data-supp-info-image="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs44221-026-00724-8/MediaObjects/44221_2026_724_Fig4_ESM.jpg">Extended Data Fig. 1 Spatiotemporal changes in hydrological and atmospheric drought across non-nested large catchments.</a></h3><div class="c-article-supplementary__description" data-component="thumbnail-container"><p><b>a</b>, Spatial distribution of catchment-scale trends in the annual mean of the six-month standardized streamflow index (SSI) during 1981–2019. <b>b</b>, Monthly percentage of land area affected by hydrological drought, defined as SSI &lt; –1. <b>c</b>, Spatial distribution of catchment-scale trends in the annual mean of the six-month standardized precipitation evapotranspiration index (SPEI) during 1981–2019. <b>d</b>, Monthly percentage of land area affected by atmospheric drought, defined as SPEI &lt; –1, derived from four observational dataset combinations (CHIRPS–GLEAM, CHIRPS–hPET, MSWEP–GLEAM and MSWEP–hPET). Map colours indicate trend magnitude in z-units per year, with negative and positive values indicating drying and wetting, respectively. Inset pie charts in <b>a</b> and <b>c</b> show the fractions of catchments exhibiting significant drying (dark red), non-significant drying (light red), non-significant wetting (light blue) and significant wetting (dark blue), using a significance threshold of <i>p</i> &lt; 0.05. Dashed lines in <b>b</b> and <b>d</b> indicate linear trends fitted to calendar-year mean drought-area percentages. Basemap data in <b>a</b> and <b>c</b> come from Natural Earth (<a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com">https://www.naturalearthdata.com</a>).</p></div></div><div class="c-article-supplementary__item js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="supp-item" id="Fig5"><h3 class="c-article-supplementary__title u-h3"><a data-test="supp-info-link" class="print-link" data-track="call_to_action" data-track-action="view supplementary info" data-track-context="Extended data" data-track-label="extended data fig. 2 spatial trends in hydrologica" href="/articles/s44221-026-00724-8/figures/5" data-supp-info-image="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs44221-026-00724-8/MediaObjects/44221_2026_724_Fig5_ESM.jpg">Extended Data Fig. 2 Spatial trends in hydrological and atmospheric drought indices across non-nested small catchments.</a></h3><div class="c-article-supplementary__description" data-component="thumbnail-container"><p><b>a</b>,<b>b</b>, Spatial distributions of catchment-scale trends in the annual mean of the six-month standardized streamflow index (SSI; <b>a</b>) and standardized precipitation evapotranspiration index (SPEI; <b>b</b>) during 1981–2019. SPEI trends represent the mean of the available product-specific trends from four observational dataset combinations (CHIRPS–GLEAM, CHIRPS–hPET, MSWEP–GLEAM and MSWEP–hPET). Map colours indicate trend magnitude in z-units per year, with negative and positive values indicating drying and wetting, respectively. Inset pie charts show the fractions of catchments exhibiting significant drying (dark red), non-significant drying (light red), non-significant wetting (light blue) and significant wetting (dark blue), using a significance threshold of <i>p</i> &lt; 0.05. Basemap data come from Natural Earth (<a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com">https://www.naturalearthdata.com</a>).</p></div></div><div class="c-article-supplementary__item js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="supp-item" id="Fig6"><h3 class="c-article-supplementary__title u-h3"><a data-test="supp-info-link" class="print-link" data-track="call_to_action" data-track-action="view supplementary info" data-track-context="Extended data" data-track-label="extended data fig. 3 regional-scale trends in drou" href="/articles/s44221-026-00724-8/figures/6" data-supp-info-image="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs44221-026-00724-8/MediaObjects/44221_2026_724_Fig6_ESM.jpg">Extended Data Fig. 3 Regional-scale trends in drought frequency and magnitude across IPCC AR6 WGI reference regions (1981–2019).</a></h3><div class="c-article-supplementary__description" data-component="thumbnail-container"><p>Each hexagon represents one of the IPCC AR6 WGI land reference regions, positioned at its approximate geographical location. a–b, Regional trends in hydrological drought frequency (<b>a</b>) and magnitude (<b>b</b>), expressed as the change in drought months per year. <b>c</b>–<b>d</b>, Regional trends in atmospheric drought frequency (<b>c</b>) and magnitude (<b>d</b>), expressed in z-units yr⁻<sup>1</sup>. White hexagons indicate regions with insufficient data coverage for robust trend estimation. Figure adapted from ref. <sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 91" title="Rowlingson, B. IPCC hex maps. Gitlab &#xA;                https://gitlab.com/b-rowlingson/ipcc&#xA;                &#xA;               (2021)." href="/articles/s44221-026-00724-8#ref-CR91" id="ref-link-section-d15889064e1961">91</a></sup> under a Creative Commons license <a href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>.</p></div></div><div class="c-article-supplementary__item js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="supp-item" id="Fig7"><h3 class="c-article-supplementary__title u-h3"><a data-test="supp-info-link" class="print-link" data-track="call_to_action" data-track-action="view supplementary info" data-track-context="Extended data" data-track-label="extended data fig. 4 spatial consistency of trend " href="/articles/s44221-026-00724-8/figures/7" data-supp-info-image="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs44221-026-00724-8/MediaObjects/44221_2026_724_Fig7_ESM.jpg">Extended Data Fig. 4 Spatial consistency of trend directions between SPEI and SSI, and between atmospheric and hydrological drought characteristics (1981–2019).</a></h3><div class="c-article-supplementary__description" data-component="thumbnail-container"><p>Each marker represents a catchment, colored according to the two-dimensional combination of trend directions in <b>a</b>, SSI and SPEI trends, <b>b</b>, hydrological and atmospheric drought frequency trends, and <b>c</b>, hydrological and atmospheric drought magnitude trends. The inset color legend defines the four quadrants corresponding to the possible combinations of positive and negative trend directions for each pair of indices. Basemap data from Natural Earth (<a href="http://www.naturalearthdata.com">https://www.naturalearthdata.com</a>) with IPCC AR6 WGI land reference regions (grey outlines) from ref. <sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 88" title="Iturbide, M. et al. An update of IPCC climate reference regions for subcontinental analysis of climate model data: definition and aggregated datasets. Earth Syst. Sci. Data 12, 2959–2970 (2020)." href="/articles/s44221-026-00724-8#ref-CR88" id="ref-link-section-d15889064e2010">88</a></sup> under a Creative Commons license <a href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>.</p></div></div><div class="c-article-supplementary__item js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="supp-item" id="Fig8"><h3 class="c-article-supplementary__title u-h3"><a data-test="supp-info-link" class="print-link" data-track="call_to_action" data-track-action="view supplementary info" data-track-context="Extended data" data-track-label="extended data fig. 5 asymmetric hydrological respo" href="/articles/s44221-026-00724-8/figures/8" data-supp-info-image="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs44221-026-00724-8/MediaObjects/44221_2026_724_Fig8_ESM.jpg">Extended Data Fig. 5 Asymmetric hydrological responses to changes in atmospheric drought frequency and magnitude.</a></h3><div class="c-article-supplementary__description" data-component="thumbnail-container"><p><b>a</b>,<b>b</b>, Spatial distribution of hydrological responses to increasing (<b>a</b>) and decreasing (<b>b</b>) atmospheric drought frequency. <b>c</b>,<b>d</b>, Corresponding responses to increasing (<b>c</b>) and decreasing (<b>d</b>) atmospheric drought magnitude. BHRI was calculated separately from the paired atmospheric and hydrological trends in each drought characteristic (see Methods). Catchments were classified as directionally decoupled, weakly responsive, approximately proportional or amplified, corresponding to BHRI values below 0, from 0 to 0.45, from 0.45 to 0.55 and from 0.55 to 1, respectively. Pie charts show the percentage of classified catchments in each response category. Increases in atmospheric drought frequency and magnitude were dominated by weak or directionally decoupled hydrological responses, together accounting for 62% and 63% of catchments, respectively. By contrast, decreases in either atmospheric drought characteristic produced amplified hydrological responses in 55% of catchments. Grey points denote catchments outside the corresponding trend direction or standard BHRI classification. The same near-zero trend criteria as in Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/articles/s44221-026-00724-8#Fig3">3</a> were applied. Basemap data from Natural Earth (<a href="http://www.naturalearthdata.com">https://www.naturalearthdata.com</a>).</p></div></div></div></div></div></section><section data-title="Supplementary information"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec22-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec22">Supplementary information</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec22-content"><div data-test="supplementary-info"><div class="c-article-supplementary__item" data-test="supp-item" id="MOESM1"><h3 class="c-article-supplementary__title u-h3"><a data-test="supp-info-link" class="print-link" data-track="call_to_action" data-track-action="view supplementary info" data-track-context="Supplementary information" data-track-label="supplementary information" href="https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs44221-026-00724-8/MediaObjects/44221_2026_724_MOESM1_ESM.pdf" data-supp-info-image="">Supplementary Information (download PDF <svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-download-medium"></use></svg>)</a></h3><div class="c-article-supplementary__description" data-component="thumbnail-container"><p>Supplementary Figs. 1–37 and Tables 1–6, including all figure and table legends.</p></div></div></div></div></div></section><section data-title="Source data"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec23-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Sec23">Source data</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec23-content"><div data-test="supplementary-info"><div class="c-article-supplementary__item" data-test="supp-item" id="MOESM2"><h3 class="c-article-supplementary__title u-h3"><a data-test="supp-info-link" class="print-link" data-track="call_to_action" data-track-action="view supplementary info" data-track-context="Source data" data-track-label="source data fig. 1" href="https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs44221-026-00724-8/MediaObjects/44221_2026_724_MOESM2_ESM.xlsx" data-supp-info-image="">Source Data Fig. 1 (download XLSX <svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-download-medium"></use></svg>)</a></h3><div class="c-article-supplementary__description" data-component="thumbnail-container"><p>Source data underlying Fig. 1, comprising catchment-scale SSI and SPEI trends and the corresponding monthly global mean and interquartile time series for 1981–2019. The workbook contains four worksheets corresponding to Fig. 1a–d.</p></div></div><div class="c-article-supplementary__item" data-test="supp-item" id="MOESM3"><h3 class="c-article-supplementary__title u-h3"><a data-test="supp-info-link" class="print-link" data-track="call_to_action" data-track-action="view supplementary info" data-track-context="Source data" data-track-label="source data fig. 2" href="https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs44221-026-00724-8/MediaObjects/44221_2026_724_MOESM3_ESM.xlsx" data-supp-info-image="">Source Data Fig. 2 (download XLSX <svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-download-medium"></use></svg>)</a></h3><div class="c-article-supplementary__description" data-component="thumbnail-container"><p>Source data underlying Fig. 2, comprising catchment-scale trends and annual global mean and interquartile time series of atmospheric and hydrological drought frequency and magnitude for 1981–2019. The workbook contains six worksheets corresponding to Fig. 2a–f.</p></div></div><div class="c-article-supplementary__item" data-test="supp-item" id="MOESM4"><h3 class="c-article-supplementary__title u-h3"><a data-test="supp-info-link" class="print-link" data-track="call_to_action" data-track-action="view supplementary info" data-track-context="Source data" data-track-label="source data fig. 3" href="https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs44221-026-00724-8/MediaObjects/44221_2026_724_MOESM4_ESM.xlsx" data-supp-info-image="">Source Data Fig. 3 (download XLSX <svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-download-medium"></use></svg>)</a></h3><div class="c-article-supplementary__description" data-component="thumbnail-container"><p>Source data underlying Fig. 3, comprising catchment-level BHRI and H* values, fitted drying and wetting response curves and confidence intervals, drying–wetting asymmetry, BHRI bin summaries, nonlinear fits and response-heterogeneity statistics. The workbook contains nine worksheets corresponding to Fig. 3a–f.</p></div></div></div></div></div></section><section data-title="Rights and permissions"><div class="c-article-section" id="rightslink-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="rightslink">Rights and permissions</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="rightslink-content"><p>Springer Nature or its licensor (e.g. a society or other partner) holds exclusive rights to this article under a publishing agreement with the author(s) or other rightsholder(s); author self-archiving of the accepted manuscript version of this article is solely governed by the terms of such publishing agreement and applicable law.</p><p class="c-article-rights"><a data-track="click" data-track-action="view rights and permissions" data-track-label="link" href="https://s100.copyright.com/AppDispatchServlet?title=Global%20hydrological%20drought%20diverges%20from%20atmospheric%20drying&amp;author=Yuhan%20Guo%20%E9%83%AD%E7%A6%B9%E5%90%AB%20et%20al&amp;contentID=10.1038%2Fs44221-026-00724-8&amp;copyright=The%20Author%28s%29%2C%20under%20exclusive%20licence%20to%20Springer%20Nature%20Limited&amp;publication=2731-6084&amp;publicationDate=2026-09-28&amp;publisherName=SpringerNature&amp;orderBeanReset=true">Reprints and permissions</a></p></div></div></section><section aria-labelledby="article-info" data-title="About this article"><div class="c-article-section" id="article-info-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="article-info">About this article</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="article-info-content"><div class="c-bibliographic-information"><div class="u-hide-print c-bibliographic-information__column c-bibliographic-information__column--border"><a data-crossmark="10.1038/s44221-026-00724-8" target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://crossmark.crossref.org/dialog/?doi=10.1038/s44221-026-00724-8" data-track="click" data-track-action="Click Crossmark" data-track-label="link" data-test="crossmark"><img loading="lazy" width="57" height="81" alt="Check for updates. Verify currency and authenticity via CrossMark" src="data:image/svg+xml;base64,"></a></div><div class="c-bibliographic-information__column"><h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="citeas">Cite this article</h3><p class="c-bibliographic-information__citation">Guo, Y., Xiong, J., Yang, Y. <i>et al.</i> Global hydrological drought diverges from atmospheric drying.
